{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","statement":"1.040 Project Management was taught in Spring 2004 as an undergraduate course.","source_span":"1.040 | Spring 2004 | Undergraduate"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","statement":"The instructor was Dr. Nathaniel Osgood, Senior Lecturer in the Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering at MIT.","source_span":"Dr. Nathaniel Osgood\nSenior Lecturer, Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering, MIT"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","statement":"Lectures meet 2 sessions per week for 1.5 hours per session.","source_span":"Lectures: 2 sessions / week, 1.5 hours / session"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","statement":"The course material is divided into five major sections: Project Organization, Project Planning, Project Monitoring, Project Control, and Project Learning.","source_span":"divided into five major sections (see Figure 1): Project Organization, Project Planning, Project Monitoring, Project Control, and Project Learning"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","statement":"Planning methodologies covered include the Critical Path Method (CPM), the Precedence Diagramming Method (PDM), the Program Evaluation and Review Technique (PERT), and the Graphical Evaluation and Review Technique (GERT).","source_span":"The Critical Path Method (CPM)\nThe Precedence Diagramming Method (PDM)\nThe Program Evaluation and Review Technique (PERT)\nThe Graphical Evaluation and Review Technique (GERT)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","statement":"The course uses Primavera P3 for deterministic time and resource scheduling and Primavera Monte Carlo for probabilistic time and resource scheduling.","source_span":"Primavera® P3 — for deterministic time and resource scheduling\nPrimavera® Monte Carlo — for probabilistic time and resource scheduling"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","statement":"Vensim is used in the course for system dynamics analysis.","source_span":"Vensim® — for system dynamics analysis"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","statement":"Attendance to recitations is required.","source_span":"Please note that attendance to recitations is required."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"Who taught 1.040 Project Management in Spring 2004, and what was their title?","answer":"Dr. Nathaniel Osgood, Senior Lecturer in the Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering at MIT, taught 1.040 Project Management in Spring 2004."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How many lecture sessions per week does the Spring 2004 Project Management course have, and how long is each session?","answer":"Lectures meet 2 sessions per week, each lasting 1.5 hours."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Into how many major sections is the 1.040 course material divided, and what are they?","answer":"Five sections: Project Organization, Project Planning, Project Monitoring, Project Control, and Project Learning."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Name four planning methodologies taught in the Project Planning section of 1.040.","answer":"The Critical Path Method (CPM), the Precedence Diagramming Method (PDM), the Program Evaluation and Review Technique (PERT), and the Graphical Evaluation and Review Technique (GERT)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Which software tool does the course use for deterministic time and resource scheduling, and which for probabilistic scheduling?","answer":"Primavera P3 is used for deterministic time and resource scheduling, and Primavera Monte Carlo for probabilistic time and resource scheduling."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2004_syllabus","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Is attendance mandatory for recitations in the 1.040 Project Management course?","answer":"Yes, attendance to recitations is required."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2009_assignments","statement":"Prof. Fred Moavenzadeh taught 1.040 Project Management, Spring 2009, an undergraduate course.","source_span":"Prof. Fred Moavenzadeh"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2009_assignments","statement":"Assignment 1 asks students what advice they would give the government on infrastructure investment as an economic stimulant, in no more than 500 words, due at Lecture #6.","source_span":"what advice would you give to the government with regard to investment in infrastructure as an economic stimulant?... not more than 500 words"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2009_assignments","statement":"Assignment 1 readings include two Economist articles from February 5, 2009 ('Be Careful What You Wish For' and 'Can The Centrists Hold?') and 'Lights! Water! Motion!' by Doshi, Schulman, and Gabaldon in Strategy + Business, issue 46 (2007).","source_span":"“Be Careful What You Wish For.”\nThe Economist\n, February 5, 2009.\n“Can The Centrists Hold?”\nThe Economist\n, February 5, 2009.\nDoshi, Viren, Gary Schulman, and Daniel Gabaldon. “\nLights! Water! Motion!\n.”\nStrategy + Business\n46 (2007)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2009_assignments","statement":"Assignment 2 requires a memo of approximately 500 words commenting on readings about construction finance and private participation in infrastructure delivery, due at Lecture #11.","source_span":"Your memo should be similar in length to the papers you prepared for Paper 1 (i.e., approximately 500 words)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2009_assignments","statement":"Assignment 2 readings include Michael Cooper's 'Big Ideas, Grand Plans, Modest Budgets' (The New York Times, February 14, 2009) and Robert Poole's 'Infrastructure Report Card: A Caution' (Surface Transportation Innovations 64, 2009).","source_span":"Cooper, Michael. “\nBig Ideas, Grand Plans, Modest Budgets\n.”\nThe New York Times\n, February 14, 2009.\nPoole, Robert. “Infrastructure Report Card: A Caution.”\nSurface Transportation Innovations\n64 (2009)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2009_assignments","statement":"Assignment 3 requires selecting two contrasting transportation case studies from a U.S. Department of Transportation report and writing a paper of roughly 5-10 pages; case selection is due at Lecture #15 and the paper is due at Lecture #20.","source_span":"Turn in a short paper (roughly 5-10 pages) with regard to your analysis and comparison of these two cases."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2009_assignments","statement":"Assignment 3's source reading is the AECOM Consult Team's 'Case Studies of Transportation Public-Private Partnerships in the United States,' a Federal Highway Administration report dated July 7, 2007.","source_span":"AECOM Consult Team. “Case Studies of Transportation Public-Private Partnerships in the United States.” Federal Highway Administration Report. July 7, 2007."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-040-project-management-spring-2009_assignments","statement":"Students enrolled in graduate subject 1.401J or ESD.081J must complete a term project consisting of a final report, an in-class presentation, and a 1-2 page project proposal.","source_span":"students enrolled in the graduate subject 1.401J or ESD.081J are asked to complete a term project. The term project consists of a final report and in-class presentation... Students must also submit a 1-2 page project proposal"}
